**Lesson written:** *Honest metrics is asymptotic — every sweep reveals dust you couldn't see before*

The journal entry for this session traced a month-long arc of measurement corrections — ghost completions, cancelled-run classification, signal-kill hints, panic-hook ledger cleanup, timeout-code naming, and now unclosed model calls + planning no-ops. Each fix tightened the gap between what the numbers say and what actually happened. But each fix also revealed the *next* gap — dust in a corner that was always there, invisible because bigger dust was in front of it.

The prior learnings covered individual measurement failure modes (wrong categories, synthetic pollution, one-direction checks). What's new here is the meta-pattern: the work itself is asymptotic. You never arrive at "perfectly honest metrics" — you just sweep until the remaining inaccuracies are smaller than the decisions you need to make from them. The house is never clean; you stop when the dust can't distort the next move.
